PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Core problem: minimal n extending the 6 ballots so a k=100 W with W∩{a,b,c,x}=∅ still satisfies PJR (s1) or EJR (s2). Group of all 6 is 3-cohesive on {a,b,c}; W can supply at most 2 reps (y+z) without violating the empty-intersection condition for voter 1. No voter among the 6 approves both y and z, so EJR cannot deliver ℓ=2 to any member of the group.

PJR threshold: group ceases to be 3-cohesive exactly when 6<3n/100 ⇒ n≥201; at that point y,z plus 98 fresh candidates suffice to meet all PJR quotas. EJR threshold: group must also cease to be 2-cohesive (because no voter can personally receive 2 reps) ⇒ 6<2n/100 ⇒ n≥301.

Competing lower figures (101,167,200,300,…) ignore the full 6-voter 3-cohesive set or mis-apply the ℓ=2 quota; higher figures (398,601,…) over-apply the ℓ=1 quota or treat the axioms as identical. Thus current best pair remains (201,301), matching the cleanest arguments (cols 11,6).}

